> Also, what exactly are HACKDIR and GAMEDIR?  If they're the same thing,
> why have 2 different names?  It seems like one should be where the game
> resides and the other where the admin files are (save files, perm and
> safelock, bones, etc).

     HACKDIR is a macro in the C code, which gets compiled into the
executable.  It can be overridden at run-time via a HACKDIR environment
variable.  GAMEDIR is a makefile variable in the top directory, but
the code is compiled via a different makefile in the src subdirectory.
They should have the same value, except HACKDIR is a quoted string and
GAMEDIR isn't.

     The game and "admin" files reside in the same directory (for Unix
and most other ports as well).  The only things that might go into other
locations are the optional shell script for invoking the program--since
it has the same name as the executable, it has to go into a different
directory--and the man pages (and save files, but they don't count :-).

> One last- where does nethack save the save files and what is the name
> format for these save files?

     They go into a save subdirectory in the "playground" directory.
If you use `make install' to set up things, that subdirectory will be
created for you.  Filenames are based on the UID and the character
name, and undergo compression based on a couple of compile-time macros
specified in config.h.

     Don't make the mistake of trying to make the playground be the top
level of the source tree, because one of the first things `make install'
does is to use `rm -rf' to remove all files from the destination directory.



